        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people shopping for a battery powered water pump want one of two things: a portable pump that moves water from where it is to where it should be, or a fixed pump that pressurizes drinking water from a tank. Those are different machines, and the label on the box rarely makes that clear.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="two-kinds" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Two Kinds of Battery Powered Water Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first kind is the cordless transfer pump. It is a small self-priming pump driven by a drill-style tool battery, with a garden hose thread on the inlet and outlet. You drop the suction hose into a water heater drain, a flooded window well, a rain barrel or a stopped-up laundry sink, run the discharge hose somewhere useful, and pull the trigger. Ryobi, DEWALT and Milwaukee all sell one, and each runs on the batteries those brands already put in millions of garages.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second kind is the 12 volt DC water pump, usually a diaphragm pump, that lives permanently inside an RV, van, boat or off-grid cabin. It sits between a fresh-water tank and the faucets, senses when pressure drops because somebody opened a tap, and runs until pressure builds back up. It draws from a house battery rather than a tool pack. Shurflo and SEAFLO dominate this category, and most RVs on the road leave the factory with one of their pumps or a close copy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A third, smaller group is the 12V submersible pump, the bilge pump boaters know. It sits in the water, has no priming problem at all because it is already submerged, and pushes water up and out through a hose. It is the simplest way to dewater a hole, a boat or a low spot when you have a car or deep-cycle battery and some wire. It is not designed for drinking water and should never be used as one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The confusion comes because all three get sold as a water pump battery powered and they look similar in search results. A transfer pump will not run your RV faucets properly because it has no pressure switch. A diaphragm RV pump can move water from a bucket, but slowly, and it is not built for sand and grit. A bilge pump will drain a hole faster than either but cannot draw water up from below itself. Decide the job first, then read the rest of this guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            If what you actually need is to keep a household well pump running during an outage, none of these is the answer. That is a 240-volt load that needs a properly sized generator and a transfer switch, covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-generator-for-well-pump/">best generator for a well pump</a>. The pumps here are for moving and pressurizing modest amounts of water away from the grid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Six Pumps Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table sorts the picks by type and power source. Flow is listed only where the manufacturer's figure is well established; where it is not, the column says so rather than guessing. The potable column matters more than the flow column for anyone filling a drinking-water tank.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
