          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pick the kind of pump first. For draining water heaters, hot tubs and flooded spots, buy the cordless transfer pump that matches the batteries you already own: the Milwaukee M18 2771-20 for frequent use, the DEWALT DCE040 for DEWALT households, or the Ryobi P750 for occasional jobs. For RV and van fresh water, the Shurflo 4008-101-E65 is the quiet, proven choice and the SEAFLO 21-Series is the budget alternative.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Respect the limits. Buy for lift and hose length rather than headline GPH, keep utility pumps out of drinking water, never pump fuel with a water pump, and fuse every 12V connection. A Rule 25D bilge pump on a fused battery lead is the cheapest dewatering tool there is, as long as it stays out of potable service.
